WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.820
Gaby: Hello, John!

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:01.600
Allison: Hello, Hank!

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.020
Gaby: We are Gaby and Allison from Just Between
Us.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.740
Allison: When they asked us to do this video,
we were excited because our channel is

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.340
a comedy channel, and we rarely get real with our talk.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.500
Gaby: I get real all the time.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.650
Allison: Yeah, you’re pretty real, but I
try to stay fake.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.650
Gaby: That’s why we’re an odd couple.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.300
That’s why this works.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.120
Allison: But there’s been one topic that
a lot of people have been asking us about

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.520
that we’ve never really addressed, and we
figured that this would be a good opportunity to do it.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.680
This is my first vlog.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:28.939
Gaby: Congrats on your first vlog!

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:30.519
Allison: Thank you! I feel different.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.320
So, that question is: Why did we leave Buzzfeed?

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.879
Gaby: We left over a year ago.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:38.239
Allison: It was probably one of the scariest
decisions we’ve ever had to make.

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:39.019
Gaby: I agree.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.620
Allison: The first few months there were amazing.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.859
Gaby: I had had a writing job before, so I
was less enthused about the whole thing.

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:48.839
Allison: I don’t think you’re ever as
enthused as I am about anything.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:49.600
Gaby: That’s true.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.960
It’s very hard for me to feel joy.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.500
And I was also like 26, so it wasn’t my
first job.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.500
It wasn’t—I wasn’t like a lot of the
other people there, who were, like just out of college.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.020
Allison: Now I feel embarrassed that it was
my first job and I was 25.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.780
It wasn’t my first job, but it was my first
job writing.
Gaby: Your first writing job.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.740
Yeah, your first writing job, which is a big
deal, and it’s like a scary thing to leave.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.640
Allison: I’d done a lot of writing for free.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.480
But it was also an incredible environment,
and there were all these people our own age,

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.920
and we were all like making stuff, and I very
distinctly remember standing there during

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.540
like an all hands meeting and just thinking
it’s so sad that I can’t work here my entire life.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.700
And so that’s what was so interesting with
like after a few months went by, when like

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.711
things kind of started to change where it became clear
that maybe that wasn’t where I was supposed

00:01:31.711 --> 00:01:36.391
to be for forever, that there was some push
back about the fact that we had our own channel.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.060
We were in a sort of department where it wasn’t
super clear like what we were supposed to be doing.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.320
Gaby: And we also were like toying with, okay
well then do we get—do we get rid of Just

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.080
Between Us and commit ourselves to Buzzfeed?

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.979
But that didn’t feel right to us either.

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:52.920
Allison: We were getting interest from other
people about doing our own stuff.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.760
We wanted to prioritize Just Between Us because
that was growing, and Just Between Us is ours.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.659
The thing about jobs is that you can leave
them.

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:01.810
And so you have to
Gaby: Big time.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.850
Allison: listen to yourself, and if like at
a certain point, even if the company’s great,

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.670
even if like you had great experiences there,
if it’s no longer the right fit, like in

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.180
a relationship, then you have to break up.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.400
Gaby: When you work for a company and make
videos, everything that you make, that company owns.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.360
So for instance, you might have heard about
some people being let go from Buzzfeed, and

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.489
those people, the characters and the videos
that they—the series that they created while

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:27.609
at Buzzfeed, they’ll never be able to make
that series ever again.

00:02:27.609 --> 00:02:31.089
And that’s because that is Buzzfeed’s
intellectual property.

00:02:31.089 --> 00:02:35.940
And that began to scare me, where I was like,
well, do I give them my best ideas?

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.540
Later in life, I may want to use those ideas
for something, and I won’t be able to.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.640
And that started worrying me more and more,
and the idea of them owning anything to do

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.720
with Just Between Us started worrying me more
and more.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.879
Allison: And I remain like so grateful to
Buzzfeed, like I do not think that like we

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:52.819
would be where we are if we hadn’t had Buzzfeed.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.020
I think it was incredible exposure. It was amazing.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.900
But at the same time, you have to remember
that a company is always a company,

00:02:58.900 --> 00:02:59.960
and a company is not your friend.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.250
And so that’s confusing because you’re
working with all of your friends, and all

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.710
of the people that work there are your friends,
but the company itself is not your friend.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.480
When I went into this industry, I knew that
it was going to be very unstable, and it was

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.330
gonna be kind of chaotic, and I wouldn’t
really—I wasn’t really gonna know what

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:14.390
was gonna come next.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.810
But I think one of the issues is if you start
working for one of those big companies, you

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.640
kind of get tricked, and you’re like, “Oh,
I have stability, and I get to be creative!”

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.900
But at a certain point, you have to remember
that you need to take risks.

00:03:24.909 --> 00:03:29.600
Gaby: In the end, you only have yourself,
and you only have what you’ve made, so like,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.220
how do you know—like, to me, I kept—whenever
we were there, I would always think, how do

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.350
I know any of these companies will be here
in five years?

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.900
Why would I, like, marry myself to this thing
that I’m like, have no guarantee of it being around?

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.510
The only thing I knew would be around was
me and you.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.600
Hopefully, if we both live.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.340
Allison: That’s really
Gaby: If we both make it

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.560
Allison: seems really optimistic.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.200
But also like that’s the kind of personality
that we are, and there are so many people

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:55.149
that like, like the way that they would be
the most happy is to stay at a company and

00:03:55.149 --> 00:03:57.800
work there long term, but it just wasn’t
right for us.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.140
And I think that that’s almost like what
we want to say the most, is like just listen

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.260
to your gut, and listen to what feels right
for you.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:06.040
And like, the other side isn’t roses.
Gaby: Yeah.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.780
Allison: Like, work, it’s constant stress.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.849
We’re constantly upset, but we’ve been
able to work on projects and like strive towards

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:15.430
projects that are like so much bigger than
I think that what we could have done if we

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.160
stayed there, and so much more our own.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.700
This feels so uncomfortable to be talking
sincerely into a camera.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.420
Gaby: Yeah, I’m really excited watching you do
it.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.700
Allison: I’m like breaking out in hives.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.220
Where are the jokes?

